{
  "result": "A city is never entirely asleep. Even at four in the morning, someone is rinsing a coffee cup, someone is waiting for a bus, and someone is standing at a window, wondering whether tomorrow will be different. Streetlights hold their small circles of gold as if keeping promises until sunrise.\n\nWe usually describe places by their landmarks: towers, bridges, museums, the impressive things that survive on postcards. But a place also consists of quieter arrangements: which bakery opens first, where rain gathers, how the crossing signal sounds, which neighbor leaves a bowl of water outside for dogs during summer afternoons.\n\nThese details rarely announce themselves as important. They become important through repetition. A familiar doorway can steady a difficult day. The smell of bread can return someone to a childhood kitchen. A tree planted before your birth can shade the bench where you finally decide to make that overdue call.\n\nPerhaps belonging is less about possession than attention. You do not need to own a street to notice its seasons. You can learn the names of its weeds, recognize the footsteps upstairs, or remember that the elderly man at the corner prefers conversation to help carrying his groceries back home.\n\nBy morning, the city resumes its louder story. Engines start. Doors slam. Headlines compete for alarm. Yet underneath all that urgency, the smaller story continues: ordinary people making ordinary gestures that render life slightly more bearable. Sometimes hope is simply this: a light left on, and somebody remembering your name.",
